	SOURCES OF DATA:


	GNIS:	United States Geological Survey (USGS) "Geographic Names
		Information System", Denver, July 1984.
	EDUS:	USGS pamphlet,	"Elevations  and Distances in the United
		States", 1980 printing (USGPO 341-618-35).
	Ormes:	Robert	Ormes,	"Guide to the Colorado	Mountains",  6th
		edition, 1974.
	B/L:	Borneman/Lampert,   "A	Climbing   Guide  to  Colorado's
		Fourteeners", 1st edition, 1978.
	NAUS:	National Atlas of the United States, 1970.


	MEANINGS, DERIVATION, AND ACCURACY:


	Latitude, Longitude:

	  Numbers represent  degrees, minutes, and seconds of arc.  They
	  are  from the GNIS  where  available;	 exceptions  are  listed
	  below.  The GNIS was	created	 by  digitizing	 best  available
	  maps,	 usually  7.5-minute  topological  maps.  United  States
	  National  Map Accuracy  Standards  state that, at the scale of
	  such maps  (1:24000),	 "not more than 10% of the points tested
	  shall be in error...	by more than 1/50 inch,	 measured on the
	  publication scale".

	  The  7.5-minute  map for the NW corner  of  Colorado	measured
	  43.76cm wide, or 17.23in.  Thus the worst  east-west error for
	  the  Colorado	 peaks would be (0.02 / 17.23 * 7.5 * 60) = 0.52
	  seconds of arc.  On nine maps measured, the height varied from
	  57.44cm  (22.61in)  to  57.78cm.  The worst  case  north-south
	  error would then be (0.02 / 22.61 * 7.5 * 60) = 0.40	seconds.
	  Both ways, 1/50 inch at 1:24000 scale represents 40 feet.

	  Of course, the GNIS  digitizing  process  probably  added some
	  error as  well.  Of seven  peaks  measured  as a check,  eight
	  coordinates  were the same,  five  were off by 1 second  after
	  rounding to the nearest  second, and one (longitude  for Mount
	  Columbia)  was off by exactly  one minute of arc  (probably  a
	  typo in the GNIS; correct value shown here).

	  North and South  Massive, South Elbert,  Tabeguache  Mountain,
	  Mount	 Sneffels, and the Colorado  corner points were measured
	  by hand from 7.5-minute topo maps, based on offsets divided by
	  widths  or  heights  of maps.	 Measurements  were  made to the
	  nearest 0.03cm and results rounded to the nearest second.  The
	  Colorado boundaries are apparently not as simple as they seem!

	  White Mountain and Mount Shasta  locations were taken from the
	  NAUS.	 They were given in tenths of minutes,	so the	inherent
	  error in the numbers above is 3 seconds of arc.

	Altitude:

	  Altitudes,  in feet above mean sea level,  were taken from the
	  EDUS where  available,  since GNIS altitudes are in some cases
	  to  benchmarks  a few feet below the peak.  In the other cases
	  they were taken from the GNIS or B/L, as available.

	Name of Peak:

	  The GNIS and EDUS names were used since they had no conflicts.
	  However,  Ormes  and B/L say  "Mount  Shavano",  not  "Shavano
	  Peak";  "Kit  Carson  Peak", not "Kit  Carson  Mountain";  and
	  "Ellingwood Peak", not "Ellingwood  Point".  Ormes says "South
	  Maroon Peak", not "Maroon Peak".

	  High points generally considered sub-peaks are marked "(sub)".
	  Mount	 Cameron  is a	sub-peak  of Mount  Lincoln;  Ellingwood
	  Point, of Blanca  Peak; and  Conundrum  Peak, of Castle  Peak.
	  There are other  local high points  above 14000 feet which are
	  closer to their main	peaks, so  typically  not named, and not
	  listed here.

	Range:

	  Main	ranges are  shown,  based on reading  maps.  "Sangre  de
	  Cristo" is abbreviated "S de Cristo".

	GN, ED, Or, BL:

	  These  columns  compare  altitudes  and peak names between the
	  four   sources.  If  a  number   appears,  it  is  the  least-
	  significant  digit or digits of the altitude from that source,
	  where different from the Altitude  column.  "M" means the peak
	  is missing  from that  source's  list.  "n" means that  source
	  calls the peak by a different name, as described above.


	OTHER COMMENTS:

	  Every	 7.5-minute  map  measured was	slightly  (less than 1%)
	  smaller than you would expect based on first	principles  (the
	  scale,  and the size of the  earth).	The  older  the map, the
	  worse	 the  error.  Apparently  they	shrink	after  printing!
	  However, scaling measurements accordingly accounts for this.

